Package: baysesp
Title: Bayesian Accuracy of Multiple Diagnostic Tests under Partial Verification
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Giovanni", "Tinervia", , "giovannitinervia9@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0000-3928-2998"))
Description: Bayesian estimation of the sensitivity, specificity and predictive
    values of one or more binary diagnostic tests performed on the same subjects,
    when the disease status is ascertained by the reference standard only for a
    subset of subjects (partial verification), which biases the estimates computed
    from verified subjects alone (Begg and Greenes (1983) <doi:10.2307/2530820>).
    Following Martinez et al. (2006) <doi:10.1016/j.csda.2005.12.021> and Buzoianu
    and Kadane (2008) <doi:10.1002/sim.3099>, it supports joint modeling of
    several tests, accuracy of combination rules (any, all, at least k positive),
    comparisons between tests, structured models for conditional dependence
    (Dendukuri and Joseph (2001) <doi:10.1111/j.0006-341X.2001.00158.x>),
    non-ignorable verification (Kosinski and Barnhart (2003)
    <doi:10.1111/1541-0420.00019>) and explicit sensitivity analyses for
    quantities that the data cannot identify. Models are written in 'Stan'
    (Carpenter et al. (2017) <doi:10.18637/jss.v076.i01>) and fitted with the
    'rstan' interface or, optionally, with 'cmdstanr'.
